\begin{center}
$\lambda$-Rings (Formal preliminaries)
\end{center}

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.sec.1.occ2}\subsection*{\S 1. Definitions}

A \emph{$\lambda$-ring}\SGAAuthorFootnote{*}{In this seminar we instead say \emph{pre-$\lambda$-ring} (V 2.1), reserving the name $\lambda$-ring for those satisfying the additional condition on page 3 (cf V 2.4).} is a commutative ring $K$ with unit, equipped with a family of maps
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-1.occ2}\begin{equation}\tag{1.1}
\lambda^i:K\longrightarrow K
\end{equation}
($i$ an integer $\geq 0$) satisfying the following conditions:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-2.occ2}\begin{equation}\tag{1.2}
\lambda^0x=1,\qquad \lambda^1x=x,\qquad
\lambda^n(x+y)=\sum_{i=0}^n\lambda^ix\cdot\lambda^{n-i}y.
\end{equation}
For every $x\in K$, put
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-3.occ2}\begin{equation}\tag{1.3}
\lambda_t(x)=\sum_{n\geq 0}\lambda^n(x)t^n\in K[[t]] ;
\end{equation}
the preceding relations then express that $x\longmapsto\lambda_t(x)$ is a homomorphism from the additive group $K$ to the multiplicative group $1+K[[t]]^+$ of formal series over $K$ with augmentation $1$, lifting the canonical homomorphism $1+\sum_{i>0}x_it^i\longrightarrow x_1$.

Thus, on the ring $\Z$, there exists a unique $\lambda$-structure compatible with its ring structure and such that

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 35  |  folio 22  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{22}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-4}\begin{equation}\tag{1.4}
\lambda_t(1)=1+t.
\end{equation}
We then have
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-5}\begin{equation}\tag{1.5}
\lambda_t(n)=(1+t)^n,
\end{equation}
hence $\lambda^i(n)=\binom{n}{i}$.

The notion of a $\lambda$-homomorphism of $\lambda$-rings is clear; an \emph{augmented $\lambda$-ring} is a $\lambda$-ring equipped with a $\lambda$-homomorphism to $\Z$.

To define the notion of a \emph{special $\lambda$-ring}, the following considerations are useful. Let $k$ be a commutative ring with unit, and let $K=1+k[[t]]^+$ be the group of formal series with coefficients in $k$ and augmentation $1$; we shall introduce on it a $\lambda$-ring law whose additive structure is multiplication of formal series. The multiplication of this ring will be denoted by $f\circ g$. It is completely determined by the condition that the coefficients $c_n=c_n(f\circ g)$ of $f\circ g$ are given by \emph{universal} polynomials with integer coefficients in the coefficients $a_i=c_i(f)$ and $b_i=c_i(g)$ of $f$ and $g$, respectively:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-6}\begin{equation}\tag{1.6}
c_n=P_n(a_1,\ldots,a_n;b_1,\ldots,b_n),
\end{equation}
that it is distributive with respect to the ``addition'' $fg$, and is given for linear factors by:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-7}\begin{equation}\tag{1.7}
(1+at)\circ(1+bt)=1+abt.
\end{equation}
The $P_n$ are calculated by calculations of symmetric functions; $P_n$ is isobaric of weight $n$ in the $a_i$ and isobaric of weight $n$ in the $b_i$ ($a_i$ and $b_i$ having weight $i$), and is moreover symmetric in $a=(a_i)$ and $b=(b_i)$. Likewise, the $\lambda^i(f)$ are determined unambiguously by the condition that

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 36  |  folio 23  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{23}
the coefficients $c_n(\lambda^i(f))=c_n\text{ of }\lambda^i(f)$ are calculated using \emph{universal} polynomials with integer coefficients in the coefficients $c_n(f)=a_n$ of $f$:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-8}\begin{equation}\tag{1.8}
c_n=P_{i,n}(a_1,\ldots,a_{in}),
\end{equation}
that relations (1.2) hold, and that, for $i>1$, we have:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-9}\begin{equation}\tag{1.9}
\lambda^i(1+at)=1
\end{equation}
($1$ is the zero element of the ring $K=1+k[[t]]^+$). The $P_{i,n}$ are again calculated by calculations of symmetric functions, and we find that $P_{i,n}$ is an isobaric polynomial of weight $in$ in the $a_i$. Equipped with the preceding operations, $K$ becomes a $\lambda$-ring; its zero element is $1$, and its unit element is $1+t$. We also note the relation:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-7bis}\begin{equation}\tag{1.7 bis}
(1+at)\circ f=f(at)
\end{equation}

Now let $K$ be an arbitrary $\lambda$-ring. We say that $K$ is \emph{special} when the additive homomorphism $\lambda_t$ from $K$ to $1+K[[t]]^+$ is a $\lambda$-homomorphism. This therefore means that we have the formulas:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-10}\begin{equation}\tag{1.10}
\lambda_t(1)=1+t,\quad
\lambda_t(xy)=\lambda_t(x)\circ\lambda_t(y),\quad
\lambda_t(\lambda^i(x))=\lambda^i(\lambda_t(x)),
\end{equation}
or, explicitly:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-11}\begin{equation}\tag{1.11}
\begin{cases}
\lambda^i(1)=0 & \text{for }i>1\\
\lambda^n(xy)=P_n(\lambda^1x,\ldots,\lambda^n x;\lambda^1y,\ldots,\lambda^n y)\\
\lambda^n(\lambda^i(x))=P_{i,n}(\lambda^1x,\ldots,\lambda^{in} x),
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where the $P_n$ and $P_{i,n}$ are the universal polynomials considered above. In particular, formulas (1.5) follow. (We can show that the $\lambda$-ring $1+k[[t]]^+$ constructed from a commutative ring $k$ is spe%
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 37  |  folio 24  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{24}%
cial; but we shall not need this fact).

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.sec.2.occ2}\subsection*{\S 2. Examples}

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.ex.2-1}1) Let $G$ be a group and $k$ a commutative ring; we consider the group $\underline{K}(G)$, the quotient of the free group generated by the classes of representations of $G$ in $k$-modules of finite type by the subgroup generated by elements of the form $(\rho\oplus\rho')-(\rho)-(\rho')$ (we write $\oplus$ for direct sum). Tensor product and exterior powers define a $\lambda$-ring structure on $\underline{K}(G)$. We can also pass to the quotient by elements of the form $(\rho)-(\rho')-(\rho'')$, where $\rho$ is a representation that is an extension of $\rho'$ by $\rho''$, trivial as an extension of $k$-modules. We obtain the group $\underline{K}_r(G)$ of reduced classes of representations of $G$, and we find that the $\lambda$-ring law passes to the quotient.

When $k$ is an \emph{algebraically closed field of characteristic} $0$\SGAAuthorFootnote{*}{It is unnecessary for $k$ to be algebraically closed, cf. (VI 3.3) for a still more general result (including all those that will be used in the present report).}, we can show that $\underline{K}(G)$, and a fortiori $\underline{K}_r(G)$, is a \emph{special} $\lambda$-ring; this no longer holds when the characteristic is $\neq 0$, but nevertheless, when $k$ is algebraically closed\SGAAuthorFootMark{*}, $\underline{K}_r(G)$ is still a special $\lambda$-ring. To prove this, we reduce to the case where $G$ is a product $\Gl(m,k)\times\Gl(n,k)$ and only rational representations of $G$ are considered; our assertion follows from the \emph{explicit determination} of $\underline{K}_r(G)$ \emph{to be made below} (example 3); finally, when $k$ has characteristic $0$, complete reducibility of the rational representations of $G$ shows that $\underline{K}(G)=\underline{K}_r(G)$.

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 38  |  folio 25  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{25}
Variants of the preceding example: $G$ is an algebraic group defined over $k$, and only the rational representations of $G$ defined over $k$ are considered; we can consider a topological group, or a Lie group, and continuous representations, etc\dots\SGAAuthorFootnote{1}{Other examples of $\lambda$-rings can be constructed, for example the set of equivalence classes of quadratic forms, the set of classes of representations of a Lie algebra, etc\dots We promise that we shall not need all these examples to treat the Riemann--Roch theorem!}

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.ex.2-2}2) Let $X$ be an algebraic variety, irreducible or not, defined over the field $k$. We can consider the quotient of the free group generated by the classes of vector bundles on $X$ (defined over $k$) by the subgroup generated by elements of the form $(E\oplus E')-(E)-(E')$, on which the $\lambda$-ring operations will be defined by tensor product and exterior powers. When $X$ is complete, this is a free group generated by the indecomposable vector bundles on $X$ (by Krull--Remak--Schmidt); knowing this ring and its basis is equivalent to the complete classification of vector bundles on $X$ and knowing the elementary tensor operations on those bundles. When $k$ is algebraically closed of characteristic $0$, all the other tensor operations on classes of bundles are then known, using what follows in 3). We can make a more drastic passage to the quotient by the group generated by the elements $(E)-(E')-(E'')$, where $E$ is a vector bundle that is an extension of $E'$ by $E''$; we then obtain a $\lambda$-ring, denoted by $\underline{K}(\xi(X))$, where $\xi(X)$ denotes the additive category of vector bundles on $X$ defined over $k$. Using the results of example 1), we show that, when $k$ is algebraically closed\SGAAuthorFootnote{*}{Unnecessary condition, cf. the preceding footnote.}, the $\lambda$-ring $\underline{K}(\xi(X))$ is special, and the same holds for the

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 39  |  folio 26  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{26}%
first $\lambda$-ring defined here, if the characteristic of $k$ is $0$, but not in the general case.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.ex.2-3}3) \emph{Determination of $\underline{K}(G)$ and of $\underline{K}_r(G)$ in certain important cases}: We suppose that the field $k$ is algebraically closed, that the group $G$ is algebraic, \emph{affine connected} and ``globally \emph{reductive}'' (the radical of $G$ is isomorphic to $k^{*s}$), and we consider only rational linear representations. In characteristic $0$, the complete reducibility of the rational linear representations of $G$ shows that $\underline{K}(G)=\underline{K}_r(G)$ is the free group generated by the classes of irreducible rational representations. Now, if $T$ is a maximal torus of $G$, we have $\underline{K}(T)=\underline{K}_r(T)=\Z(\That)$, the algebra of the group $\That$ of rational characters of $T$; we have $\That\simeq\Z^r$ if $r$ is the rank of $G$ (this is independent of the characteristic). The Weyl group $W$ operates on $T$, hence on $\underline{K}(T)$, in a way that depends only on the operations of $W$ on the free group $\That$. We have an evident homomorphism of $\lambda$-rings, deduced from the injection of $T$ into $G$:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-13}\begin{equation}\tag{1.13}
\underline{K}_r(G)\longrightarrow \underline{K}_r(T)^W=\underline{K}(T)^W,
\end{equation}
where $\underline{K}(T)^W$ denotes the set of fixed points of $W$ in $\underline{K}(T)$. The theory of linear representations of $G$\SGAAuthorFootnote{*}{Cf.\ Séminaire Chevalley 1956/58 Groupes de Lie algébriques (École Normale Supérieure).} then proves the following theorem:

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.thm.1-1}\textbf{Theorem 1.1.} \emph{The homomorphism} (1.13) \emph{is an isomorphism.}

We thus see that $\underline{K}_r(G)$, once the ``type'' of $G$ is known (characterized by $W$ operating on a group $\Z^r$), does not depend on the characteristic, and that it is a special $\lambda$-ring. We have a canonical basis of $\underline{K}_r(G)$ corresponding to the orbits of $W$ in $\That$; another basis is obtained as follows:
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 40  |  folio 27  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{27}%
let $(\rho_i)_{1\leq i\leq r'}$ be the representation classes of $G$ corresponding to the \emph{fundamental} representations of $G/\rad G$, and let $(\sigma_i)_{1\leq i\leq r''}$ be a basis of the group of rational homomorphisms from $G$ to $k^*$; we thus have $r'+r''=r$ (the rank of $G$).

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.cor.unnamed}\textbf{Corollary:} \emph{The ring $\underline{K}_r(G)$ is identified with the subring}
\[
\Z[\rho_1,\ldots,\rho_{r'};\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_{r''};\sigma_1^{-1},\ldots,\sigma_{r''}^{-1}]
\]
\emph{of the field of rational functions in the $\rho_i$ and the $\sigma_j$ with coefficients in $\Q$}\SGAAuthorFootnote{*}{The derived group $G'$ of $G$ must be assumed simply connected for this statement to be correct.}.

In particular, $\underline{K}_r(G)$ has no divisors of $0$; if $G=\prod_i\Gl(n_i,k)$, the ring $\underline{K}_r(G)$ is made explicit as follows: let $\rho_i$ be the representation of $G$ deduced from the identity representation of $\Gl(n_i,k)$; if we then consider the field of rational functions over $\Q$ in indeterminates $\lambda^j(\rho_i)$ ($1\leq j\leq n_i$), then $\underline{K}_r(G)$ is identified with the subring generated by these indeterminates and the inverses of the $\lambda^{n_i}(\rho_i)$. This justifies our assertion that, at least in characteristic $0$, the operations $\lambda^i$ allow all tensor operations on vector bundles to be reconstructed; this is still true over an algebraically closed field of arbitrary characteristic, provided reduced classes are taken.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.rem.unnamed}\textbf{Remarks:} a) In example 1), the ring $\underline{K}(G)$ is augmented, the augmentation being defined by the degree of the representations, and, by passage to the quotient, this augmentation defines an augmentation on $\underline{K}_r(G)$. Likewise, the $\lambda$-rings considered in example 2) are augmented when $X$ is $k$-irreducible; in $\underline{K}(\xi(X))$, the augmentation is defined by the \emph{rank of a vector bundle}, that is, the dimension of its fibres.

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 41  |  folio 28  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{28}%
b)
When the field $k$ is not algebraically closed, it is plausible that $\underline{K}(G)$ is a special $\lambda$-ring in characteristic $0$, and likewise for $\underline{K}_r(G)$ in all characteristics.

c) In nonzero characteristic, theorem 1.1 was kindly supplied to me by Chevalley.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.sec.3.occ2}\subsection*{\S 3. The $\lambda$-ring defined by a graded ring}

Let $A$ be a commutative graded ring with unit; for simplicity we shall assume that the degrees of $A$ are positive and that $A^0=\Z$. We denote by $\Ahat$ the product of the $A^i$ (it is a commutative ring with unit), and by $\Ahp$ the kernel of the evident augmentation $\Ahat\to A^0=\Z$. Then $1+\Ahp$ is a subgroup of the multiplicative group of invertible elements of $\Ahat$. Consider the abelian group
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-14}\begin{equation}\tag{1.14}
\widetilde A=\Z\times(1+\Ahp),
\end{equation}
whose elements will be denoted $[n,x]$ with $n\in\Z$ and $x=1+\sum_{i\geq 1}x^i\in1+\Ahp$ ($x^i\in A^i$). We shall write this group additively, thus:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-15}\begin{equation}\tag{1.15}
[n,x]+[n',x']=[n+n',xx'].
\end{equation}
The zero element of this group is $[0,1]$. We are going to introduce on it an augmented $\lambda$-ring structure, compatible with its additive structure, the augmentation being $\varepsilon:[n,x]\longrightarrow n$. The product $[m,x][n,y]$ is completely characterized by being written $[mn,x^n y^m(x*y)]$, where $x*y$ is an element of $1+\Ahp$ whose components are expressed as universal polynomials with integer coefficients in the $x^i$ and the $y^i$:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-16}\begin{equation}\tag{1.16}
(x*y)^i=Q_i(x^1,\ldots,x^i;y^1,\ldots,y^i)\qquad (i\geq1)
\end{equation}
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 42  |  folio 29  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{29}%
the
polynomial $Q_i$ being isobaric of weight $i$. Moreover, $x*y$ must be distributive with respect to the ``addition'' $xy$ and, for ``linear'' factors, must reduce to the following:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-17}\begin{equation}\tag{1.17}
[1,1+x^1][1,1+y^1]=[1,1+x^1+y^1].
\end{equation}
The polynomials $Q_i$ are evaluated by calculations with elementary symmetric functions (this is the calculation of the Chern classes of a tensor product of vector bundles by means of the Chern classes $x^i$ and $y^i$ of the factors and their respective dimensions $m$ and $n$). From (1.17) we deduce:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-17bis}\begin{equation}\tag{1.17 bis}
(1+x^1)*(1+y^1)=(1+x^1+y^1)/(1+x^1)(1+y^1),
\end{equation}
and an arbitrary product $x*y$ is calculated term by term by decomposing ``formally'' $x$ into a product $\prod_{i=1}^n(1+\alpha_i)$ and $y$ into a product $\prod_{i=1}^n(1+\beta_i)$, where the $\alpha_i$ and the $\beta_i$ have degree $1$.

In this way, $\widetilde A$ becomes an augmented commutative ring, with a unit $[1,1]$, and it can be interpreted as the ring obtained by adjoining a unit to the ring $1+\Ahp$. Moreover, on $\widetilde A$, we define a \emph{filtration} by putting $\widetilde A^0=\widetilde A$ and denoting by $\widetilde A^n$ the set of the $[0,x]$ with $x^i=0$ for $1\leq i<n$; this filtration is compatible with the product, for we have
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-18}\begin{equation}\tag{1.18}
\left(1+\sum_{i\geq m}x^i\right)*\left(1+\sum_{j\geq n}y^j\right)
=1-\frac{(m+n-1)!}{(m-1)!(n-1)!}x^m y^n+\cdots,
\end{equation}
the unwritten terms having degree $>m+n$. This formula shows that the graded ring $G(\widetilde A)$ associated with $\widetilde A$ is identified with $A$ from the additive point of view, but not from the multiplicative point of view; we can only say that the map $\eta'$ from $A$ to $G(\widetilde A)$ defined by
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 43  |  folio 30  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{30}%
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-19}\begin{equation}\tag{1.19}
\eta'(x^i)=(-1)^{i-1}(i-1)!\,x^i\qquad (x^i\in A^i)
\end{equation}
is a \emph{ring homomorphism}.

We introduce on $\widetilde A$ operations $\lambda^i$ satisfying the axioms of $\lambda$-rings (\S 1, formulas (1.2)), well determined by the condition that the components of $\lambda^i[0,x]$ be deduced from the components $x^i$ of $x$ by \emph{universal} polynomials with integer coefficients
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-20}\begin{equation}\tag{1.20}
\lambda^i[0,x]=[0,\lambda^i x]\qquad (\lambda^i x)^{(n)}=Q_{i,n}(x^1,\ldots,x^n)
\end{equation}
($Q_{i,n}$ is isobaric of weight $n$), and that we have
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-21}\begin{equation}\tag{1.21}
\lambda^i[1,1+x^1]=0\qquad\text{for }i>1.
\end{equation}
The polynomials $Q_{i,n}$ are evaluated by a calculation with elementary symmetric functions, which is the calculation of the Chern classes of the exterior powers of a vector bundle, by means of the Chern classes and the rank of the latter. We then find that $\widetilde A$ is a \emph{special} augmented $\lambda$-ring and that the $\widetilde A^i$ are stable under the operations $\lambda^j$.

The augmented $\lambda$-ring $\widetilde A$ may be considered as a functor in $A$; consequently, the principal automorphism
\[
x=\sum_{i\geq0}x^i\longrightarrow \check x=\sum_{i\geq0}(-1)^i x^i
\]
of $A$ defines an automorphism of $\widetilde A$, denoted by the same symbol $\check{\ }$.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.prop.1-2}\textbf{Proposition 1.2.} \emph{For $x\in\widetilde A$ of the form $[n,1+x^1+\cdots+x^n]$} (we therefore have $x^i=0$ for $i>n$), \emph{we have $\lambda^i(x)=0$ for $i>n$, we have $\lambda^n(x)=[1,1+x^1]$, and finally}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-22}\begin{equation}\tag{1.22}
\sum_{i=0}^n(-1)^i\lambda^i(x)=[0,1-(n-1)!x^n+\cdots]
\end{equation}
From this proposition and from the fact that the $\widetilde A^i$ are stable under the operations $\lambda^j$, we deduce that formula (1.22) is valid whenever $\varepsilon(x)=n$, whether or not we have $x^i=0$ for $i>n$.

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 44  |  folio 31  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{31}%
Formula
(1.22) admits the following generalizations: let $K$ be any $\lambda$-ring, otherwise arbitrary; for $x\in K$, put
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-23}\begin{equation}\tag{1.23}
\gamma^n(x)=(-1)^n\sum_{i=0}^n(-1)^i\lambda^i(x+n)=\lambda^n(x+n-1)
\end{equation}
(the identity of the last two members follows from an immediate induction). In particular, we have $\gamma^0(x)=1$ and $\gamma^1(x)=x$; next put:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-24}\begin{equation}\tag{1.24}
\gamma_t(x)=\sum_{n\geq0}\gamma^n(x)t^n\in K[[t]]
\end{equation}
whence we deduce the formula:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-25}\begin{equation}\tag{1.25}
\gamma_t(x)=\lambda_{t/(1-t)}(x),
\end{equation}
which is equivalent to
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-25bis}\begin{equation}\tag{1.25 bis}
\lambda_s(x)=\gamma_{s/(1+s)}(x).
\end{equation}
This proves that the $\lambda^i$ are expressed by means of the $\gamma^i$ and that $\gamma_t(x+y)=\gamma_t(x)\gamma_t(y)$, and consequently that the maps $\gamma^i$ define a new $\lambda$-ring structure on $K$. Formula (1.22) then shows that, for $K=\widetilde A$ and every $x\in\widetilde A$, we have
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-22bis}\begin{equation}\tag{1.22 bis}
\gamma^n(x-\varepsilon(x))=[0,1+(-1)^{n-1}(n-1)!x^n+\cdots]
\end{equation}
and consequently, the \emph{component} $x^n$ \emph{is obtained, up to the factor} $(-1)^{n-1}(n-1)!$, \emph{by reducing modulo} $\widetilde A^{n+1}$ \emph{the element} $\gamma^n(x-\varepsilon(x))$ \emph{of} $\widetilde A^n$.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.the-chern-homomorphism}\paragraph{The Chern homomorphism:}
For every graded ring $A$ satisfying the preceding conditions, we are going to define a homomorphism of augmented rings:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-26}\begin{equation}\tag{1.26}
\ch:\widetilde A\longrightarrow \widehat{A\otimes\Q}.
\end{equation}
This homomorphism is defined unambiguously by the conditions that it be an additive homomorphism, take $1$ to $1$, be such that the com%
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 45  |  folio 32  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{32}%
ponents of $\ch(x)$ in degree $>0$ be given by universal isobaric polynomials with rational coefficients in the components of $x$, and that
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-27}\begin{equation}\tag{1.27}
\ch([1,1+x^1])=\exp x^1=\sum_{n\geq0}(x^1)^n/n!.
\end{equation}
We then immediately obtain the explicit expression for $\ch$: let $\eta$ be the additive endomorphism of $\widehat{A\otimes\Q}$ defined, for $x^i$ of degree $i$, by
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-28}\begin{equation}\tag{1.28}
\eta(x^i)=1/(-1)^{i-1}(i-1)!\,x^i
\end{equation}
we then have
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-29}\begin{equation}\tag{1.29}
\ch\left([n,1+\sum_{i\geq1}x^i]\right)=n+\eta\left(\log\left(1+\sum_{i\geq1}x^i\right)\right).
\end{equation}
As we can ``invert'' this formula, at least when $A$ has only finitely many homogeneous components, we see that, in this case, we have an \emph{isomorphism} $\ch$ \emph{from the ring} $\widetilde A\otimes\Q$ \emph{onto the ring} $\widehat{A\otimes\Q}$ (which completely elucidates the structure of $\widetilde A\otimes\Q$).

Recall that, according to Hirzebruch [1], a formal series $f\in\Q[[t]]$ defines, by universal polynomial formulas, an additive homomorphism $1+\Ahp\longrightarrow 1+(\widehat{A\otimes\Q})^+$, denoted $\mathcal T_f$; when $f(t)=t/(1-\exp(-t))$, we simply put $\mathcal T_f=\mathcal T$ ($\mathcal T$ is the initial of Todd). We extend the definition of $\mathcal T(x)$ to $x\in\widetilde A$. This said, we have the following result:

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.prop.1-3}\textbf{Proposition 1.3.} \emph{Let $N\in\widetilde A$ be of the form $[q,1+N^1+\cdots+N^q]$} (we have $N^i=0$ for $i>q$). \emph{If we put:}
\[
\lambda_{-1}(N)=\sum_{i\geq0}(-1)^i\lambda^i(N)=\sum_{i=0}^{q}(-1)^i\lambda^i(N),
\]
\emph{we shall have:}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-30}\begin{equation}\tag{1.30}
\ch(\lambda_{-1}(N))=(-1)^q N^q\mathcal T(\check N)^{-1},
\end{equation}
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 46  |  folio 33  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{33}%
\emph{which is equivalent to:}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-30bis}\begin{equation}\tag{1.30 bis}
\ch(\lambda_{-1}(\check N))=N^q\mathcal T(N)^{-1}.
\end{equation}

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.sec.4.occ2}\subsection*{\S 4. The operations $\lambda^p(N,x)$}

Let $A$ be the ring of formal series, with coefficients in $\Z$, in two infinite sequences of indeterminates, denoted $\lambda^iN$ and $\lambda^i x$ ($i\geq1$). Put $\lambda^1N=N$ and $\lambda^1x=x$, and introduce on $A$ the unique special $\lambda$-ring structure for which we have $\lambda^i(N)=\lambda^iN$ and $\lambda^i(x)=\lambda^ix$ and for which the operations $\lambda^i$ are ``continuous'' (if we restricted ourselves to \emph{polynomials} in indeterminates $\lambda^iN$ and $\lambda^ix$, we would have the ``free $\lambda$-ring generated by $N$ and $x$''; our ring $A$ is a completion of it). We can then form
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-31}\begin{equation}\tag{1.31}
\lambda_{-1}(N)=\sum_{i\geq0}(-1)^i\lambda^iN,
\end{equation}
which is a formal series with constant term $1$, hence invertible; we can therefore define an element $\lambda^p(N,x)$ of $A$ unambiguously by putting
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-32}\begin{equation}\tag{1.32}
\lambda^p(N,x)\lambda_{-1}(N)=\lambda^p(x\lambda_{-1}(N))
\end{equation}

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.thm.1-4}\textbf{Theorem 1.4.} \emph{Let $J_q$ be the closed ideal of $A$ generated by the $\lambda^iN$ with $i>q$; identify $A/J_q$ with the ring of formal series in the $\lambda^i x$ and the $\lambda^jN$ ($1\leq j\leq q$). Then $\lambda^p(N,x)$ reduced modulo $J_q$ is a} polynomial, \emph{isobaric of weight $p$ with respect to the variables $\lambda^i x$.}

\emph{Let $N$ and $F$ be two vector spaces of finite dimensions $q,q'$ over an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $0$; let $N^{(p)}$ be the kernel of the map} $(a_1,\ldots,a_p)\longrightarrow a_1+\cdots+a_p$ from $N^p$ \emph{to} $N$.
We
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 47  |  folio 34  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{34}%
\emph{let the symmetric group $\mathfrak S_p$ operate on $N^p$, hence on $N^{(p)}$ hence on $\Lambda(N^{(p)})$, then on $\bigotimes^p F$ and on $\Lambda(N^{(p)})\otimes\bigotimes^p F$. Consider this vector space as a graded module over $\mathfrak S_p\times\Gl(N)\times\Gl(F)$ and take its ``alternating part'', which is a graded module over $\Gl(N)\times\Gl(F)\eqqcolon G$. We then have}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.1-33}\begin{equation}\tag{1.33}
\lambda^p(N,F)=\underline{E}_G\left(\left(\Lambda(N^{(p)})\otimes\bigotimes^p F\right)^{\mathrm{alt}}\right).
\end{equation}

N.B. If $M$ is a complex of $G$-modules, we denote by $\underline{E}_G(M)$ the alternating sum in $\underline{K}(G)$ of the classes of the components of $M$; this is the Euler--Poincaré characteristic of $M$; in formula (1.33), the first member $\lambda^p(N,F)$ denotes the element of the ring $\underline{K}(G)$ obtained by substituting for the variables $\lambda^iN$ and $\lambda^iF$ the classes in $\underline{K}(G)$ of the $G$-modules $\Lambda^i(N)$ and $\Lambda^i(F)$ respectively.

\textbf{Proof:} By expanding the second member of (1.32) by the second formula (1.11), we see that the first assertion is proved if we prove the analogous assertion obtained by putting $x=1$, i.e. if we prove that, in the $\lambda$-ring $\Z[\lambda^1N,\ldots,\lambda^qN]$ where $\lambda^iN=0$ for $i>q$, the element $\lambda^p(\lambda_{-1}(N))$ is divisible by $\lambda_{-1}(N)$. A priori, the quotient $\lambda^p(N,1)=\lambda^p(\lambda_{-1}(N))/\lambda_{-1}(N)$ is a formal series contained in the field of fractions $K$ of the ring of formal series in the $\lambda^iN$ for $1\leq i\leq q$. On the other hand, also denote by $N$ a vector space of dimension $q$ over an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $0$, and consider the element $\ell^p(N)=\underline{E}_G((\Lambda(N^{(p)}))^{\mathrm{alt}})\in \underline{K}(G)$, where we now have $G=\Gl(N)$. We know (\S 2, theorem 1.1) that $\underline{K}(G)$ is identified
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 48  |  folio 35  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{35}%
with the
subring of $K$ generated by the $\lambda^iN$ for $1\leq i\leq q$ and by the inverse of $\lambda^qN$. On the other hand, we readily verify in this ring that we have $\ell^p(N)\lambda_{-1}(N)=\lambda^p(\lambda_{-1}(N))$. As we are in a large field $K$ and $\lambda_{-1}(N)\neq0$, we conclude that $\ell^p(N)=\lambda^p(N,1)$; both members are in the intersection of $\underline{K}(G)$ and the ring of formal series in the $\lambda^iN$, and are therefore polynomials in the $\lambda^iN$.

To prove (1.33) in the general case, we verify that the second member satisfies in $\underline{K}(G)$ the formula analogous to (1.32), that is, its product by $\lambda_{-1}(N)$ is $\lambda^p(F\lambda_{-1}(N))$. Likewise, the product of $\lambda^p(N,F)\in \underline{K}(G)$ by $\lambda_{-1}(N)$ is $\lambda^p(F\lambda_{-1}(N))$, as follows from the fact that $\underline{K}(G)$ is \emph{special}. As $\underline{K}(G)$ is an integral domain (cf.\ \S 2) and $\lambda_{-1}(N)\neq0$ (same reference), we have indeed proved formula (1.33). q.e.d.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.rem.unnamed.occ2}\textbf{Remarks:} a) When the dimension $q'$ of $F$ is $\geq p$, then (1.33) characterizes the polynomial $\lambda^p(N,x)$ in the $\lambda^iN$ ($1\leq i\leq q$) and the $\lambda^jx$ ($1\leq j\leq p$).

b) It is plausible that (1.33) still holds when the field $k$ is not algebraically closed\SGAAuthorFootnote{*}{This is indeed proved by the same method.}. This question would have to be resolved to prove the Riemann--Roch theorem over a field of characteristic $0$ that is not algebraically closed by the method we are going to explain. Moreover, the passage from an $\mathfrak S_p$-module to its alternating part has a reasonable meaning only when the base field has characteristic $0$.

Now let $K$ be any \emph{special} $\lambda$-ring and let $N$ be an
