\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.chapter-ii-classes-of-coherent-algebraic-sheaves.occ2}\section*{CHAPTER II}
\begin{center}
Classes of coherent algebraic sheaves\par
and Chern classes
\end{center}

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.sec.1.occ3}\subsection*{\S 1. Chow's theory}

Throughout this chapter, we assume a \emph{base field} $k$ fixed, which we shall assume algebraically closed, for simplicity. A large part of what follows, and perhaps all of it, is nevertheless valid without this restriction. The algebraic spaces and regular maps considered will be defined over $k$.

An algebraic space is called \emph{quasi-projective} if it is isomorphic to a locally closed subset of a projective space.

Let $X$ be a connected nonsingular quasi-projective variety of dimension $n$. We denote by $A(X)$, and call the \emph{Chow ring} of $X$, the graded ring of classes of cycles on $X$ modulo rational equivalence. ($A^i(X)$ consists of the classes of cycles of dimension $n-i$). The fact that $A(X)$
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 52  |  folio 39  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{39}%
is indeed a graded ring follows from the following theorem of Chow:

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.thm.2-1}\textbf{Theorem 2.1.}\SGAAuthorFootnote{*}{As a result of a misunderstanding on the author's part, the theorem states more than is currently known. In fact, only Chow's ``moving lemma'' is used, saying that two cycles can be moved within their classes so that their intersection has no excess; cf.\ Séminaire Chevalley 1958, Anneau de Chow et applications.} \emph{Every cycle on $X$ is rationally equivalent to a nonsingular cycle. Given elements of $A^i(X)$ and $A^j(X)$, we can find nonsingular representatives of these elements, say $Y^{n-i}$ and $Z^{n-j}$, which meet transversely everywhere.}

(A cycle is called nonsingular if its components are nonsingular subvarieties. We say that two cycles meet transversely everywhere if every component of one meets every component of the other transversely everywhere, i.e. if the two intersecting varieties are nonsingular at their points of intersection and their tangent varieties there are in general position \dots)

Let $f$ be a morphism (i.e. a regular map) from $X$ to $Y$ ($X$ and $Y$ being quasi-projective and nonsingular); then $f$ defines (using the preceding theorem) a homomorphism of graded rings:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-1.occ2}\begin{equation}\tag{2.1}
f^*:A(Y)\longrightarrow A(X)
\end{equation}
(\emph{inverse image of cycle classes}). Thus $A(X)$ becomes a contravariant functor in $X$.

Let $f$ be a \emph{proper} morphism from $X^n$ to $Y^m$ (i.e. in Weil's terminology, the graph of $f$ is complete over $Y$, cf.\ [3]). Then $f$ also defines an additive homomorphism
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-2}\begin{equation}\tag{2.2}
f_*:A(X^n)\longrightarrow A(Y^m)
\end{equation}
preserving the dimension of cycles, and therefore increasing the degree by $m-n$.

% --- Source pages 47--75 ---
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 53  |  folio 40  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{40}%
Thus, with respect to proper morphisms, and disregarding the multiplicative structures and gradings, $A(X)$ is a covariant functor in $X$. Let us note the classical formula
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-3.occ2}\begin{equation}\tag{2.3}
 f_*(x.f^*(y))=f_*(x).y .
\end{equation}

We shall also use Chow's theory of Chern classes. It consists in attaching to every vector bundle $E$ on $X$ (still assumed quasi-projective and nonsingular) \emph{Chern classes} $C^i(E)\in A^i(X)$, ($i\geq 1$), in such a way as to satisfy the following conditions; put $C^0(E)=1$ and
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-4.occ2}\begin{equation}\tag{2.4}
 \Ctil(E)=\left[\rang E,\sum_{i\geq 0}C^i(E)\right]\in \Atil(X)
\end{equation}
(see chapter I, \S 3 for the definition of $\Atil(X)$). The conditions to be satisfied by a theory of Chern classes are then expressed as follows:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-5.occ2}\begin{equation}\tag{2.5}
 \Ctil(E)=\Ctil(E')+\Ctil(E'')
\end{equation}
if the vector bundle $E$ is an extension of $E'$ by $E''$ (putting $C(E)=\sum_{i\geq0}C^i(E)$, this condition is expressed by $C(E)=C(E').C(E'')$). Formula (2.5) allows $\Ctil$ to be extended to an additive homomorphism from $\underline{K}(\xi(X))$ (cf.\ Chap.\ I, \S 2, example 2) to $\Atil(X)$:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-6}\begin{equation}\tag{2.6}
 \Ctil:\underline{K}(\xi(X))\longrightarrow \Atil(X),
\end{equation}
and we want $\Ctil$ to be a \emph{$\lambda$-homomorphism of $\lambda$-rings}; this last condition is also written
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-7}\begin{equation}\tag{2.7}
 \Ctil(E\otimes F)=\Ctil(E)\Ctil(F)\qquad
 \Ctil(\Lambda^iE)=\lambda^i\Ctil(E),
\end{equation}
for two vector bundles $E$ and $F$ (of course, $\Ctil$ is automatically compatible with the augmentations). Let $D$ be a divisor on $X$ and $L(D)$ the vector bundle it defines; we want

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 54  |  folio 41  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{41}%
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-8}\begin{equation}\tag{2.8}
 C^1(L(D))=\ellc(D)\qquad C^i(L(D))=0\quad (i>1)
\end{equation}
(where,
for an arbitrary cycle $Z$, we denote by $\ellc(Z)$ its class in $A(X)$). Finally, we require the $C^i$ to be \emph{functorial}, i.e. that, when $f$ is a morphism from $X$ to $Y$ and $E$ is a vector bundle on $Y$, we have
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-9}\begin{equation}\tag{2.9}
 C^i(f^!(E))=f^*(C^i(E)),
\end{equation}
where $f^!(E)$ is the inverse image of $E$ on $X$. We can also write (2.9) in the following form: the notion of inverse image of a bundle defines a $\lambda$-homomorphism of augmented $\lambda$-rings
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-10}\begin{equation}\tag{2.10}
 f^!:\underline{K}(Y)\longrightarrow \underline{K}(X)\SGAAuthorFootnote{*}{The notation $f^!$ used here conflicts with that used in duality theory (cf.\ for example R. Hartshorne, Residues and Duality, Lecture Notes in Math. no.\ 20, Springer, 1966). This is why in this Seminar we replace it by the notation $f^*$.}
\end{equation}
(here we write $\underline{K}(X)$ instead of $\underline{K}(\xi(X))$), and (2.9) means that the diagram commutes:
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large,row sep=large]
K(Y) \arrow[r,"f^!"] \arrow[d,"\Ctil_Y"'] & K(X) \arrow[d,"\Ctil_X"]\\
\Atil(Y) \arrow[r,"f^*"] & \Atil(X).
\end{tikzcd}
\]
(The space on which the symbol $\Ctil$ is considered has been put as a subscript.)

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.thm.2-2}\textbf{Theorem 2.2.} \emph{There exists a theory of Chern classes satisfying conditions (2.5), (2.7), (2.8) and (2.9).}

We shall also admit the following proposition:

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.prop.2-3}\textbf{Proposition 2.3.} \emph{Let $Y$ be a closed subset of $X$ and $U$ its complement; if $x$ is an element of $A(X)$ inducing $0$ on $U$, there exists a representative of $x$ that is a cycle supported on $Y$.}

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 55  |  folio 42  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{42}%
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.cor.unnamed.occ2}\textbf{Corollary.} \emph{Let $p=\dim X-\dim Y$. Then, if $i<p$, the homomorphism $A^i(X)\longrightarrow A^i(U)$ is bijective. Every element of the kernel of $A^p(X)\longrightarrow A^p(U)$ is a linear combination of components of dimension $n-p$ of $Y$ (and is therefore proportional to $\ellc(Y)$ if $Y$ is irreducible)}

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.sec.2.occ3}\subsection*{\S 2. Definition of the Chern classes of coherent algebraic sheaves}

Let $\mathbb{C}$ be an abelian category, and let $\mathbb{C}_0$ be a subclass of $\mathbb{C}$. We denote by $K(\mathbb{C}_0)$ the quotient group of the free group generated by the classes (up to isomorphism) of elements of $\mathbb{C}_0$, by the subgroup generated by elements of the form $(E)-(E')-(E'')$, where $E$ is an \emph{extension} of $E'$ by $E''$ ($E,E',E''\in\mathbb{C}_0$). We must assume that the classes of elements of $\mathbb{C}_0$ for the isomorphism relation form a set. We denote by $\gamma_{\mathbb{C}_0}(E)$ the class of an $E\in\mathbb{C}_0$ in $K(\mathbb{C}_0)$, and omit the subscript $\mathbb{C}_0$ when there is no risk of confusion. The group $\underline{K}(\mathbb{C}_0)$ is the solution of a \emph{universal problem} concerning functions $E\longrightarrow f(E)$ defined on $\mathbb{C}_0$, with values in an arbitrary abelian group $A$, which are \emph{additive} in the sense that $f(E)=f(E')+f(E'')$ whenever $E$ is an extension of $E'$ by $E''$.

Let $F$ be an additive functor from $\mathbb{C}$ to $\mathbb{C}'$, and $\mathbb{C}'_0$ a subclass of $\mathbb{C}'$ satisfying the same condition as $\mathbb{C}_0$, such that, for every $A\in\mathbb{C}_0$, the $R^qF(A)$ are in $\mathbb{C}'_0$ and are zero for sufficiently large $q$ (we assume that injective resolutions exist in $\mathbb{C}$, so that the derived functors $R^qF$ of $F$ exist); then $F$ defines a homomorphism from $\underline{K}(\mathbb{C}_0)$ to $\underline{K}(\mathbb{C}'_0)$, still denoted $F$, by the formula
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-11}\begin{equation}\tag{2.11}
 F(\gamma_{\mathbb{C}_0}(E))=\sum_{i\geq 0}(-1)^i\gamma_{\mathbb{C}'_0}(R^iF(E)).
\end{equation}
More generally, when we have a cohomological functor $(F^i)$ from $\mathbb{C}$ to $\mathbb{C}'$
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 56  |  folio 43  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{43}%
such that,
for every $E\in\mathbb{C}_0$, the $F^i(E)$ are in $\mathbb{C}'_0$ and are zero except for finitely many, we deduce a homomorphism from $\underline{K}(\mathbb{C}_0)$ to $\underline{K}(\mathbb{C}'_0)$. These remarks extend to multifunctors; moreover, the ``classical'' spectral sequences give transitivity properties that we shall not make explicit.

In particular, when $F$ is an exact functor, formula (2.11) simplifies:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-11bis}\begin{equation}\tag{2.11 bis}
 F(\gamma_{\mathbb{C}_0}(E))=\gamma_{\mathbb{C}'_0}(F(E)).
\end{equation}
This applies in particular when $\mathbb{C}=\mathbb{C}'$, when $F$ is the identity functor, and when $\mathbb{C}_0\subset\mathbb{C}'_0$: we have a canonical homomorphism from $\underline{K}(\mathbb{C}_0)$ to $\underline{K}(\mathbb{C}'_0)$.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.thm.2-3}\textbf{Theorem 2.3.} \emph{Let $\mathbb{C}$ be an abelian category and $\mathbb{C}_0\subset\mathbb{C}_1$ two subclasses such that the classes} (up to isomorphism) \emph{of objects of $\mathbb{C}_1$ form a set. We assume that the following conditions hold}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item \emph{For every exact sequence $0\to A'\to A\to A''\to0$ in $\mathbb{C}$ such that $A$ and $A''$ are in $\mathbb{C}_0$ (resp.\ $\mathbb{C}_1$), we have $A'\in\mathbb{C}_0$ (resp.\ $A'\in\mathbb{C}_1$).}
\item \emph{Let $u:A\to B$ and $v:C\to B$ with $A,B,C\in\mathbb{C}_1$ and $u$ surjective; then there exist $L\in\mathbb{C}_0$ and homomorphisms $u':L\to C$ and $v':L\to A$ such that $u'$ is surjective and $vu'=uv'$.}
\item \emph{For every $A\in\mathbb{C}_1$, there exists an integer $d=d(A)$ such that, for every left resolution $\underline{L}$ of $A$ by objects of $\mathbb{C}_0$, we have $Z_d(\underline{L})\in\mathbb{C}_0$} ($Z_d$ denotes the cycles of dimension $d$).
\end{enumerate}
\emph{Under these conditions, the canonical homomorphism from $K(\mathbb{C}_0)$ to $K(\mathbb{C}_1)$ is an isomorphism.}

\textbf{Principle of the proof:} let $A\in\mathbb{C}_1$; by (ii) and (iii) there exists a finite resolution $\underline{L}$ of $A$ by objects of $\mathbb{C}_0$; put
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 57  |  folio 44  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{44}%
then $f(A)=\sum_{i\geq 0}(-1)^i\gamma_{\mathbb{C}_0}(L^i)$.
We show that the element of $\underline{K}(\mathbb{C}_0)$ thus constructed does not depend on the chosen resolution $\underline{L}$: given a second resolution $\underline{L}'$, we ``cover'' $\underline{L}$ and $\underline{L}'$ by a third resolution $\underline{L}''$, with \emph{surjective} homomorphisms from $\underline{L}''$ to $\underline{L}$ and $\underline{L}'$ (which is possible by (i) and (ii)); we are then reduced to proving $\underline{E}_{\mathbb{C}_0}(\underline{L})=\underline{E}_{\mathbb{C}_0}(\underline{L}'')$. Now the difference of the two is $\underline{E}_{\mathbb{C}_0}(\underline{N})$, where $\underline{N}$ is the kernel of the surjective homomorphism from $\underline{L}''$ onto $\underline{L}$, a kernel that is in $\mathbb{C}_0$ by (i). Now $\underline{N}$ is an acyclic complex in all dimensions, whence $\underline{E}_{\mathbb{C}_0}(\underline{N})=0$, whence the conclusion (N.B. $\underline{E}_{\mathbb{C}_0}(\underline{L})$, for a finite complex with values in $\mathbb{C}_0$, denotes the Euler--Poincaré characteristic $\sum_{i\geq0}(-1)^i\gamma_{\mathbb{C}_0}(L^i)$ of $\underline{L}$).

We prove analogously that the function $f:\mathbb{C}_1\longrightarrow\underline{K}(\mathbb{C}_0)$ thus constructed is \emph{additive}, and therefore defines a homomorphism from $\underline{K}(\mathbb{C}_1)$ to $\underline{K}(\mathbb{C}_0)$, and it is immediate that this homomorphism is inverse to the canonical homomorphism from $\underline{K}(\mathbb{C}_0)$ to $\underline{K}(\mathbb{C}_1)$. q.e.d.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.rem.unnamed.occ3}\textbf{Remark:} To verify (ii), it suffices to verify that $\mathbb{C}_1$ is contained in a subclass $\mathbb{C}_2$ of $\mathbb{C}$ satisfying the two conditions
\begin{enumerate}[label=(ii \alph*)]
\item Every $A\in\mathbb{C}_2$ is isomorphic to a quotient of an $L\in\mathbb{C}_0$.
\item If $u:A\to B$, with $A,B\in\mathbb{C}_2$, then $\Ker u\in\mathbb{C}_2$.
\end{enumerate}

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.cor.unnamed.occ3}\textbf{Corollary:}
\emph{Let $X$ be a quasi-projective algebraic space. Let $\cF(X)$ be the category of coherent algebraic sheaves on $X$, $\xi(X)$ the category of locally free coherent algebraic sheaves} (equivalent to the category of vector bundles on $X$), \emph{and $\cF_0(X)$ the category of coherent algebraic sheaves $F$ such that, for every $x\in X$, $F_x$ is an $\underline{O}_x$-module
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 58  |  folio 45  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{45}%
of finite cohomological dimension (we therefore have $\xi(X)\subset\cF_0(X)\subset\cF(X)$). Then the canonical homomorphism}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-12}\begin{equation}\tag{2.12}
 K(\xi(X))\longrightarrow K(\cF_0(X))
\end{equation}
\emph{is an isomorphism.}

We are going to verify the conditions of theorem 2.3. Conditions (i) and (iii) follow from the definition of cohomological dimension and from the fact that a projective module of finite type over the noetherian local ring $\underline{O}_x$ is free. To verify (ii), we shall show that $\mathbb{C}=\cF(X)$ satisfies conditions (ii a) and (ii b) of the remark. The second is trivial; it therefore remains to prove the following result:

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.prop.2-4}\textbf{Proposition 2.4. (Serre).} \emph{Let $X$ be a quasi-projective algebraic space. Then every coherent algebraic sheaf on $X$ is isomorphic to the quotient of a locally free algebraic sheaf.}

This proposition holds when $X$ is a closed subset of projective space, by [2], since, for sufficiently large $n$, $F(n)$ is generated by its sections, which means that $F$ is a quotient of $\underline{O}(-n)^k$. In the general case, $X$ is an open subset of a closed subset of a projective space, and it suffices to use the following result (due to Cartier and Serre in particular cases):

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.prop.2-5}\textbf{Proposition 2.5.} \emph{Let $F$ be a coherent algebraic sheaf on an open subset $U$ of an algebraic space $X$. Then $F$ is isomorphic to the restriction of a coherent algebraic sheaf on $X$.}

By applying Zorn's lemma to the open subsets containing $U$ to which $F$ can be extended, we are reduced to the case where $X$ is affine. Let $\overline F$ be the
alge%

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 59  |  folio 46  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{46}%
braic sheaf on $X$ whose sections on an open subset $V$ are the sections of $F$ on $U\cap V$. We readily find that $\overline F$ is \emph{quasi-coherent} (i.e. defined on every affine open subset $V$ -- hence here on all of $X$ -- by a module, of finite type or otherwise, over the coordinate ring of the open subset in question). This is immediate when $U$ itself is affine, for $\overline F$ is then defined on the open subset $V$ by $\Gamma(U\cap V,F)$ considered as a module over $\Gamma(V,\underline{O}_X)$. In the general case, $U$ is a finite union of affine open subsets $U_i$, so $\overline F$ is the kernel of an evident homomorphism of quasi-coherent sheaves:
\[
 \sum_i \overline{F|U_i}\longrightarrow \sum_{i,j}\overline{F|U_i\cap U_j}
\]
and is therefore itself quasi-coherent. As $X$ is assumed affine, $\overline F$ is therefore generated by its sections. As $\overline F|U=F$ is coherent, there exist finitely many sections of $\overline F$ generating $\overline F|U$. These sections generate a coherent subsheaf of $\overline F$, whose restriction to $U$ is isomorphic to $F$.
\begin{flushright}q.e.d.\end{flushright}

Now assume $X$ quasi-projective and \emph{nonsingular}. Then, by the syzygy theorem, we have, with the notation of the corollary to theorem 2.3, $\cF_0(X)=\cF(X)$. In this case we therefore have:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-13}\begin{equation}\tag{2.13}
 \underline{K}(\xi(X))=\underline{K}(\cF(X)).
\end{equation}
We can express this isomorphism as follows:

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.cor.2}\textbf{Corollary 2 to theorem 2.3.} \emph{Let $X$ be a nonsingular quasi-projective variety. For every abelian group $A$, there is a one-to-one correspondence between additive functions $f$ of vector bundles on $X$, with values in $A$, and additive functions $g$ of coherent algebraic sheaves on $X$, with values in $A$. To every $f$ corresponds the unique $g$ such that we have:}
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 60  |  folio 47  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{47}%
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-14}\begin{equation}\tag{2.14}
 f(E)=g(\underline{O}_X(E))
\end{equation}
\emph{for every vector bundle $E$ on $X$, denoting by $\underline{O}_X(E)$ the sheaf of germs of regular sections of $E$ on $X$.}

To calculate $g(F)$ for an arbitrary coherent algebraic sheaf, we consider a finite resolution of $F$ by sheaves of the form $\underline{O}_X(E_i)$, and we then have:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-14bis}\begin{equation}\tag{2.14 bis}
 g(F)=\sum_{i\geq0}(-1)^if(E_i).
\end{equation}

In particular, when $X$ is connected, we considered in \S 1 an additive function $\Ctil(E)$ of the vector bundle $E$, with values in the group $\Atil=\Atil(X)$. We deduce from it an additive function of coherent sheaves, say $\Ctil(F)$, and, by passing to components, functions $F\longrightarrow C^i(F)\in A^i(X)$. The $C^i(F)$ are called the \emph{Chern classes of the coherent sheaf} $F$; they are theoretically calculated from the Chern classes of vector bundles by (2.14 bis) (where, however, we must pass to multiplicative notation in the second member). As for the component of $\Ctil(F)$ along $\Z$, it is the \emph{rank} of the sheaf $F$, which corresponds to the rank of a module over an integral domain: when $X$ is affine (a case to which we can always reduce), $F$ is defined by a module of finite type over the coordinate ring of $X$, and we take the rank of this module\dots.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.sec.3.occ3}\subsection*{\S 3. Functorial generalities on $\underline{K}(X)$}

When $X$ is an algebraic space, we put, for brevity, $\underline{K}(X)=\underline{K}(\cF(X))$, and, when $X$ is nonsingular and quasi-projective, we identify this group with $\underline{K}(\xi(X))$ by corollary 2 to theorem 2.3. As $\underline{K}(\xi(X))$ is
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 61  |  folio 48  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{48}%
not
only an abelian group, but a $\lambda$-ring, it follows by transport of structure that $\underline{K}(X)$ is also a $\lambda$-ring, and is therefore equipped with a multiplicative structure and maps $\lambda^i$. It is important to define these structures directly, without passing to vector bundles; this will be done below.

If $E$ is a vector bundle on $X$, we denote by $\gamma(E)$ its class in $\underline{K}(X)$; if $F$ is a coherent algebraic sheaf on $X$, $\gamma(F)$ likewise denotes its class in $\underline{K}(X)$. If $Y$ is an irreducible subvariety of $X$, we put $\gamma(Y)=\gamma(\underline{O}_Y)$, where $\underline{O}_Y$ denotes the sheaf of local rings of $Y$, considered as a coherent algebraic sheaf on $X$. We define the symbol $\gamma(Y)$ by linearity when $Y$ is an arbitrary cycle on $X$. When there is a risk of confusion, we shall write $\gamma_X$ for $\gamma$.

Let $X$ be an arbitrary algebraic space. We introduce a \emph{filtration} on $\underline{K}(X)$, denoting by $\underline{K}(X)^i$ the subgroup of $\underline{K}(X)$ generated by the $\gamma(F)$ with dim.supp.\ $F\leq n-i$ (where $n$ is the dimension of $X$). From Serre's ``dévissage lemma'', in the form given in [3], follows the result:

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.prop.2-6}\textbf{Proposition 2.6.} \emph{The group $\underline{K}(X)^i$ is generated by the $\gamma(Y)$, where $Y$ runs through the set of subvarieties of $X$ of dimension $\leq n-i$. If $X$ is irreducible, we have $G^0(\underline{K}(X))=\Z$.}

The latter isomorphism is given by the homomorphism from $\underline{K}(X)$ to $\Z$ defined by means of the notion of \emph{rank} of a coherent algebraic sheaf.

Now assume $X$ \emph{nonsingular}. Following the developments
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 62  |  folio 49  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{49}%
at the beginning of \S 2, we
define a multiplication on $\underline{K}(X)$ by putting
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-15}\begin{equation}\tag{2.15}
 \gamma(F)\gamma(G)=\sum_{i\geq0}(-1)^i\gamma(\underline{\Tor}_i(F,G))
\end{equation}
(the $\underline{\Tor}_i$ being taken, of course, with respect to the local rings of $X$). The exact sequence of the $\underline{\Tor}_i$ shows that formula (2.15) does define a biadditive map from $\underline{K}(X)\times\underline{K}(X)$ to $\underline{K}(X)$; associativity follows from the spectral sequence of multiple $\Tor$. We also verify in this way that the product of several factors can be calculated by the formula:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-16}\begin{equation}\tag{2.16}
 \gamma(F_1)....\gamma(F_p)=\sum_{i\geq0}(-1)^i\gamma(\underline{\Tor}_i(F_1,\ldots,F_p))
\end{equation}
where, in the second member, the $\underline{\Tor}_i$ are ``simultaneous $\underline{\Tor}$'', calculated by simultaneous projective resolution of the arguments $F_i$.

If, in (2.15), we assume $G$ \emph{locally free}, we find:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-15bis}\begin{equation}\tag{2.15 bis}
 \gamma(F)\gamma(G)=\gamma(F\otimes G),
\end{equation}
which shows in particular that $\gamma(\underline{O}_X)$ is the unit element of $\underline{K}(X)$, and that the canonical homomorphism from $\underline{K}(\xi(X))$ to $\underline{K}(X)$ is a ring homomorphism.

Formula (2.15) is evidently suggested by Serre's ``$\Tor$ formula'' in intersection theory. This formula proves the second part of the following proposition, the first following immediately from formula (2.15) and a calculation of local $\underline{\Tor}_i$:

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.prop.2-7}\textbf{Proposition 2.7.} \emph{Let $X$ be a nonsingular variety, and $Y$ and $Z$ two cycles in $X$. If $Y$ and $Z$ meet transversely everywhere, we have:}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-17}\begin{equation}\tag{2.17}
 \gamma(Y)\gamma(Z)=\gamma(Y.Z).
\end{equation}
\emph{If $Y$ and $Z$ are homogeneous and have respective dimensions $n-i$ and $n-j$, and if they meet without an excess component, we then have:}
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 63  |  folio 50  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{50}%
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-18}\begin{equation}\tag{2.18}
 \gamma(Y)\gamma(Z)=\gamma(Y.Z)\qquad\mathrm{mod.}\ \underline K(X)^{i+j+1}.
\end{equation}

We
must take care that equality does not hold in general in (2.18). We shall see in \S 4 that, when $X$ is \emph{nonsingular and quasi-projective, the ring structure of $\underline K(X)$ is compatible with its filtration and that the associated graded ring $G(\underline K(X))$ is identified with a quotient of $A(X)$ by a torsion subgroup (perhaps always reduced to $0$\SGAAuthorFootnote{*}{No; for a counterexample cf.\ this Seminar XIV 4.7.})}.

I know how to define the $\lambda^i(F)$ directly in terms of sheaves only when the field $k$ has characteristic $0$ (this is the reason that has prevented us from proving the Riemann--Roch theorem when $k$ is not of characteristic $0$). Let $F$ be a coherent algebraic sheaf on $X$ on which the group $\mathfrak S_p$ of permutations of $p$ letters operates; we then denote by $(F)^{\mathrm{alt}}$ the set of the $x\in F$ such that $s.x=\varepsilon(s)x$ for every $s\in\mathfrak S_p$ ($\varepsilon(s)$ is the \emph{signature} of $s$). This is evidently a coherent algebraic subsheaf of $F$. This said, we have:

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.thm.2-8}\textbf{Theorem 2.8.} \emph{Let $X$ be a nonsingular quasi-projective variety over the field} $k$ of characteristic $0$. \emph{Let $F$ be a coherent algebraic sheaf on $X$; we then have:}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-19}\begin{equation}\tag{2.19}
 \lambda^p(\gamma(F))=\sum_{i\geq0}(-1)^i\gamma\left((\underline{\Tor}_i(F,F,\ldots,F))^{\mathrm{alt}}\right)
\end{equation}

N.B. It is clear that the group $\mathfrak S_p$ acts on the sheaf $\underline{\Tor}_i(F,\ldots,F)$.

\emph{Principle of the proof}: we consider a finite resolution $\underline L$ of $F$ by locally free sheaves $L_i$, and use the following general lemma:

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 64  |  folio 51  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{51}%
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.lem.2-9}\textbf{Lemma 2.9.} \emph{Let $\underline L=(L_i)$ be a graded locally free coherent algebraic sheaf, with finitely many nonzero components. In $\underline K(X)$ we have the formula:}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-20}\begin{equation}\tag{2.20}
 \underline E\left((\overset{p}{\otimes}\underline L)^{\mathrm{alt}}\right)=\wedge^p(\underline E(\underline L)).
\end{equation}
In this formula, we consider $\overset{p}{\otimes}\underline L$ as a sheaf on which the group $\mathfrak S_p$ operates, taking the gradings into account (which therefore introduce signs in the well-known way, cf.\ Cartan--Eilenberg); moreover, if $\underline M$ is a graded coherent algebraic sheaf on $X$, we put $\underline E(\underline M)=\sum_{i\geq0}(-1)^i\gamma(M_i)$.

To prove formula (2.20), we are reduced to proving the analogous formula in $\underline K(G)$, where $G$ is an algebraic group (a product of groups $\operatorname{Gl}(n,k)$, if desired) and $\underline L$ a $G$-module of finite dimension over $k$ (cf.\ Chap.\ I, \S 2, example 1, for the definition of $\underline K(G)$). In this case, this formula has already been used in theorem 1.4 to show that the second member of (1.33) satisfies the same functional equation as the first member; its verification is elementary.
\begin{flushright}q.e.d.\end{flushright}

Let $f$ be a morphism from $X$ to $Y$, where $Y$ is an algebraic space. The notion of inverse image of a vector bundle defines a homomorphism $f^!:\underline K(\xi(Y))\longrightarrow\underline K(\xi(X))$ of $\lambda$-rings. Identifying vector bundles with locally free algebraic sheaves, the notion of inverse image of a vector bundle corresponds to the notion of inverse image of an algebraic sheaf (already used in [3, no.\ 8]). When $Y$ is nonsingular, we define more generally a group homomorphism:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-21}\begin{equation}\tag{2.21}
 f^!:\underline K(Y)\longrightarrow\underline K(X)
\end{equation}
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 65  |  folio 52  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{52}%
by a
formula involving an alternating sum of $\Tor$, inspired by the developments at the beginning of \S 2, which the reader will make explicit. The following diagram then commutes:
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large,row sep=large]
\underline K(\xi(Y)) \arrow[r,"f^!"] \arrow[d] & \underline K(\xi(X)) \arrow[d]\\
\underline K(Y) \arrow[r,"f^!"] & \underline K(X){,}
\end{tikzcd}
\]
which shows in particular, when $X$ and $Y$ are both nonsingular and quasi-projective, that the two definitions of $f^!$ coincide under the identification of $\underline K(X)$ with $\underline K(\xi(X))$ and of $\underline K(Y)$ with $\underline K(\xi(Y))$.

Another case in which a homomorphism (2.21) can be defined, without $Y$ necessarily being nonsingular, is that in which the functor $F\to f^!(F)$ from $\cF(Y)$ to $\cF(X)$ is exact, i.e.\ when the local rings of $X$ are \emph{flat} modules over the local rings of $Y$. It then suffices to put $f^!(\gamma_Y(F))=\gamma_X(f^!(F))$.
This case occurs in particular when $X$ is a locally trivial fibre space over $Y$ (to see this, we are reduced to the case of a product $X=T\times Y$). Note that we also verify that, in this case, we have $f^!(\underline{O}_Z)=\underline{O}_{f^{-1}(Z)}$ if $Z$ is an irreducible subvariety of $Y$, whence:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-22}\begin{equation}\tag{2.22}
 f^!(\gamma_Y(Z))=\gamma_X(f^{-1}(Z))
\end{equation}
\emph{if $X$ is ``flat'' over $Y$}. Without this last condition, formula (2.22) is in general incorrect, but we have the following analogue of proposition 2.7:

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.prop.2-8}\textbf{Proposition 2.8.}
\emph{Let $f:X\longrightarrow Y$ be a morphism of nonsingular varieties, and $Z$ a cycle on $Y$. If $Z$ is everywhere transverse to $f$, formula}
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 66  |  folio 53  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{53}%
\emph{(2.22) is correct; if $Z$ is homogeneous of codimension $i$ and $f^{-1}(Z)$ has no excess component, we have:}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-23}\begin{equation}\tag{2.23}
 f^!(\gamma_Y(Z))\equiv\gamma_X(f^{-1}(Z)).\qquad\mathrm{mod.}\ \underline K(X)^{i+1}
\end{equation}
Proof analogous to that of proposition 2.7.

Finally, let us mention that we shall see in \S 4 that, \emph{when $X$ and $Y$ are quasi-projective and nonsingular, $f^!$ is compatible with the filtrations of $\underline K(X)$ and of $\underline K(Y)$}.

Again let $X$ and $Y$ be two arbitrary algebraic spaces, and $f$ a \emph{proper} morphism from $X$ to $Y$. For every coherent algebraic sheaf $F$ on $X$, the direct image sheaf $f_!(F)$ is coherent algebraic, and the same holds more generally for the sheaves $R^qf_!(F)$ (cf.\ [3]). Recall that, by definition, $R^qf_!(F)$ is the sheaf on $Y$ associated with the presheaf assigning to an open subset $U$ the group $H^q(f^{-1}(U),F)$; in particular, we have:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-24}\begin{equation}\tag{2.24}
 R^0f_!(F)=f_!(F),\qquad R^qf_!(F)=0\quad(q>\dim X),
\end{equation}
moreover:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-25}\begin{equation}\tag{2.25}
 \Gamma(U,R^qf_!(F))=H^q(f^{-1}(U),F)
\end{equation}
\emph{if the open subset $U$ is affine}. By \S 2, we define a homomorphism from $\underline K(X)$ to $\underline K(Y)$, still denoted $f_!$, by the formula
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-26}\begin{equation}\tag{2.26}
 f_!(\gamma_X(F))=\sum_{q\geq0}(-1)^q\gamma_Y(R^qf_!(F)).
\end{equation}
Thus $\underline K(X)$ becomes a \emph{covariant functor in $X$}, with respect to \emph{proper} morphisms. The relation $(fg)_!=f_!g_!$ (just like the relation $(fg)^!=g^!f^!$ that we forgot to mention in its place) is a parti%
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 67  |  folio 54  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{54}%
cular case of the transitivity relations to which we alluded in \S 2, which follow from the spectral sequences of composite functors.

Let us note the following formula, valid for $Y$ nonsingular (and particularly easy to verify when $Y$ is also quasi-projective, by taking for $y$ the class of a vector bundle on $Y$):
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-27}\begin{equation}\tag{2.27}
 f_!(x.f^!(y))=f_!(x).y\qquad(x\in\underline K(X),\ y\in\underline K(Y)).
\end{equation}
In the particular case where the inverse images under $f$ of the points of $Y$ are finite and contained in an affine open subset\SGAAuthorFootnote{*}{This last condition is evidently a consequence of the first (something the author still did not know in 1957!).}, we find that the functor $F\longrightarrow f_!(F)$ is exact and that $R^qf_!(F)=0$ for $q>0$; in this case, we therefore have $f_!(\gamma_X(F))=\gamma_Y(f_!(F))$. In particular, if $X$ is an algebraic subspace of $Y$ and $i$ is the canonical injection from $X$ to $Y$, we have:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-28}\begin{equation}\tag{2.28}
 i_!(\gamma_X(F))=\gamma_Y(F),
\end{equation}
where, in the second member, $F$ is considered as a sheaf on $Y$ that is zero outside $X$.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.sec.4.occ3}\subsection*{\S 4. Some technical results}

Let $X$ be an algebraic space; we saw in \S 3 that the projection $p$ from $X\times k$ onto $X$ defines a homomorphism
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-29}\begin{equation}\tag{2.29}
 p^!:\underline K(X)\longrightarrow\underline K(X\times k).
\end{equation}

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.prop.2-9}\textbf{Proposition 2.9.} \emph{The homomorphism (2.29) is surjective.}

We argue by induction on $n=\dim X$; the assertion is trivial if $n<0$; assume $n\geq0$; by proposition 2.6, it suffices to
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 68  |  folio 55  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{55}%
verify that, for every irreducible closed subset $Y$ of $X\times k$, $\gamma(Y)$ is in the image of $p^!$. It suffices to continue the reasoning assuming $X$ irreducible; if $p(Y)$ is not dense in $X$, our assertion follows from the induction hypothesis; otherwise, either $Y=X\times k$ and the assertion is trivial, or $Y$ is a hypersurface in $X\times k$. Now let $U$ be an affine open subset of $X$ consisting of nonsingular points; if $D$ is the divisor on $U\times k$ induced by $Y$, we know (commutative algebra) that it is linearly equivalent to a divisor of the form $D'\times k$, where $D'$ is a divisor on $U$. Then $\gamma_{U\times k}(D)-\gamma_{U\times k}(D'\times k)$ belongs to $\underline K(U\times k)^2$ (corollary of proposition 2.10), and, by what we have already proved, this element is of the form $p^!(x')$ with $x'\in\underline K(U)$. Thus $\gamma_{U\times k}(D)=p^!(y')$ with $y'=x'+\gamma_U(D')$; but $y'$ is the restriction of an element $y$ of $\underline K(X)$ (prop.\ 2.10), and consequently the restriction of $\gamma_{X\times k}(Y)-p^!(y)$ to $U\times k$ is zero; by proposition 2.10, $\gamma_{X\times k}(Y)-p^!(y)$ comes from an element of $\underline K(X\times k-U\times k)=\underline K(X'\times k)$ with $X'=X-U$. The proof is completed by applying the induction hypothesis again.
\begin{flushright}q.e.d.\end{flushright}

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.cor.unnamed.occ4}\textbf{Corollary:} \emph{Let $X$ be an algebraic space and $p$ the projection from $X\times k^n$ onto $X$; then the homomorphism $p^!:\underline K(X)\longrightarrow\underline K(X\times k^n)$ is surjective; it is even bijective when $X$ is nonsingular.}

Induction on $n$ for the first assertion. For the second, we put $f(x)=(x,0)$, whence $pf=\underline{1}$ and $f^!p^!=1$, which proves that $p^!$ is injective.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.prop.2-10}\textbf{Proposition 2.10.} \emph{Let $X$ be an algebraic space, $U$ an open subset of $X$, and $Y$ its complement in $X$. Then the sequence of natural homomorphisms:}

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 69  |  folio 56  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{56}%
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-30}\begin{equation}\tag{2.30}
 \underline K(Y)\longrightarrow\underline K(X)\longrightarrow\underline K(U)\longrightarrow 0
\end{equation}
\emph{is exact.}

N.B. Compare with proposition 2.3.

The fact that $\underline K(X)\longrightarrow\underline K(U)$ is surjective follows immediately from proposition 2.5 (or also from proposition 2.6). It remains to prove that, if $x\in\underline K(X)$ has zero restriction to $\underline K(U)$, it is in the image of $\underline K(Y)$. For this, we return to the explicit definition of $\underline K(X)$ and $\underline K(U)$: we have two coherent algebraic sheaves $F$ and $G$ on $X$ with $x=\gamma_X(F)-\gamma_X(G)$ and $\gamma_U(F)-\gamma_U(G)=0$; this last relation means that we can find coherent sheaves $P'$ and $Q'$ on $U$, equipped with filtrations whose factors are pairwise isomorphic on $U$, and such that we have an isomorphism on $U$:
\[
 P=F+Q'\longrightarrow Q=G+P'.
\]
By proposition 2.5, the sheaves $P'$ and $Q'$ are restrictions to $U$ of coherent sheaves on $X$ (denoted by the same letters); likewise, their filtrations come from filtrations on the corresponding sheaves on $X$, by lemma 2.11 below. We then have:
\[
 \gamma_X(F)-\gamma_X(G)=(\gamma_X(P)-\gamma_X(Q))+(\gamma_X(P')-\gamma_X(Q')).
\]
Introducing the factors $P'_i$ and $Q'_j$ of the filtrations of $P'$ and $Q'$ on $X$, the last term is a sum of terms $\gamma_X(P'_i)-\gamma_X(Q'_i)$, where $P'_i|U\simeq Q'_i|U$. We are thus reduced to proving the following particular case: if $R$ and $S$ are coherent sheaves on $X$ such that $R|U\simeq S|U$, then $\gamma_X(R)-\gamma_X(S)$ is in the image of $\underline K(Y)$. Now let $T$ be the graph of the given isomorphism from $R|U$ onto $S|U$; it is a coherent subsheaf of $(R\times S)|U$, and therefore, by lemma 2.11, it is the restriction to $U$ of a coherent subsheaf,
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 70  |  folio 57  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{57}%
again
denoted $T$, of $R\times S$. We then have $\gamma_X(R)-\gamma_X(S)=(\gamma_X(R)-\gamma_X(T))-(\gamma_X(S)-\gamma_X(T))$, and it suffices to prove, for example, that the first term of the second member is in the image of $\underline K(Y)$. Now the projection $u:T\longrightarrow R$ is an isomorphism over $U$, so $\gamma_X(R)-\gamma_X(T)=\gamma_X(\operatorname{Coker}u)-\gamma_X(\operatorname{Ker}u)$, and $\operatorname{Ker}u$ and $\operatorname{Coker}u$ have their support in $Y$.

It therefore remains to prove the following lemma:

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.lem.2-11}\textbf{Lemma 2.11.} \emph{Let $X$ be an algebraic space, $F$ a coherent algebraic sheaf on $X$, $U$ an open subset of $X$, and $G$ a coherent subsheaf of $F|U$. Then $G$ is the restriction to $U$ of a coherent subsheaf of $F$.}

Let $\overline G$ be the subsheaf of $F$ whose sections on the open subset $V$ of $X$ are the sections of $F$ on $V$ whose restriction to $U\cap V$ is a section of $G$. Everything reduces to proving that this sheaf $\overline G$ is coherent. For this, we can assume $X$ and $U$ affine (for $U$ is a union of affine open subsets $U_i$, and $\overline G$ is then the intersection of the analogous sheaves $\overline{G|U_i}$). Then the coordinate ring of $U$ is a ring of fractions $A_S$ of the coordinate ring $A$ of $X$, and we verify immediately that, if $F$ is defined by the $A$-module $M$, then $F|U$ is defined by the $A_S$-module $M_S=A_S\otimes_A M$. The subsheaf $G$ of $F|U$ is then defined by a submodule $N'$ of $M_S$, and we see that $\overline G$ is precisely defined by the submodule $N$ of $M$ that is the inverse image of $N'$ under the canonical homomorphism from $M$ to $M_S$.
\begin{flushright}q.e.d.\end{flushright}

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.cor.1}\textbf{Corollary 1 to proposition 2.10:} \emph{Let $x\in\underline K(X)$. For $x$ to have filtration $\geq i$, it is necessary and sufficient that there exist a closed subset $Y$ of $X$, of dimension $\leq n-i$, such that the restriction of $x$ to $X-Y$ is zero.}

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 71  |  folio 58  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{58}%
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.cor.2.occ2}\textbf{Corollary 2.} \emph{Assume $X$ connected of dimension $n$, without singularities in dimension $n-1$. If $D$ and $D'$ are two linearly equivalent divisors on $X$, we have $\gamma(D)\equiv\gamma(D')\quad\mathrm{mod.}\ \underline K(X)^2$.}

By corollary 1, we can assume $X$ nonsingular; the corollary then follows from the general formula:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-31}\begin{equation}\tag{2.31}
 \gamma(D)=1-\gamma(L(-D))\qquad\mathrm{mod}\ \underline K(X)^2,
\end{equation}
where $L(-D)$ denotes the vector bundle of rank $1$ defined by the divisor $-D$. To verify (2.31), we write it in the form:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-31bis}\begin{equation}\tag{2.31 bis}
 \gamma(L(-D))=1-\gamma(D)\qquad\mathrm{mod}\ \underline K(X)^2,
\end{equation}
and note that both members are homomorphisms from the group of divisors to the multiplicative group of invertible elements of the ring $\underline K(X)/\underline K(X)^2$ (note that $\underline K(X)^1/\underline K(X)^2$ has square zero, which is very easily verified from the explicit formula (2.15), using corollary 1). We are thus reduced to the case where $D$ is an irreducible hypersurface. More generally, if $D$ is a hypersurface whose components are disjoint, we have the equality:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-32}\begin{equation}\tag{2.32}
 \gamma(D)=1-\gamma(L(-D))
\end{equation}
as follows from the exact sequence of sheaves
\[
0\longrightarrow\underline{O}_X(L(-D))\longrightarrow\underline{O}_X\longrightarrow\underline{O}_D\longrightarrow0.
\]

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.thm.2-12}\textbf{Theorem 2.12.} \emph{Let $X$ be a nonsingular quasi-projective variety, and $Z$ and $Z'$ two homogeneous cycles of codimension $p$ on $X$ that are linearly equivalent. We then have:}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-33}\begin{equation}\tag{2.33}
 \gamma_X(Z)\equiv\gamma_X(Z')\qquad\mathrm{mod}\ \underline K(X)^{p+1}.
\end{equation}
By hypothesis, we can find a cycle $Y$ of dimension $n-p+1$ on
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 72  |  folio 59  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{59}%
$X\times k$ ($n$ is the
dimension of $X$), and two points $a,a'\in k$ such that we have:
\[
 Z=f_a^{-1}(Y),\qquad Z'=f_{a'}^{-1}(Y),
\]
where we put $f_u(x)=(x,u)$. By formula (2.23), we then have:
\[
 \gamma_X(Z)\equiv f_a^!(\gamma_{X\times k}(Y))\qquad\mathrm{mod}\ \underline K(X)^{p+1}
\]
and an analogous formula for $\gamma_X(Z')$. It then suffices to prove that $f_a^!=f_{a'}^!$, which follows immediately from proposition 2.9.
\begin{flushright}q.e.d.\end{flushright}

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.cor.1.occ2}\textbf{Corollary 1:} \emph{The filtration of $\underline K(X)$ is compatible with its ring structure, i.e.\ we have $\underline K(X)^i\underline K(X)^j\subset\underline K(X)^{i+j}$ for all integers $i,j\geq0$. Moreover, we have a natural surjective homomorphism of graded rings:}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-34}\begin{equation}\tag{2.34}
 \varphi:A(X)\longrightarrow G(\underline K(X))
\end{equation}
(where the second member denotes the graded ring associated with $\underline K(X)$ equipped with the filtration $(\underline K(X)^i)_{i\geq0}$) \emph{defined by the condition}:
\[
 \varphi(Z^{n-p})=\gamma(Z^{n-p})\qquad\mathrm{mod}\ \underline K(X)^{p+1}.
\]
This corollary follows immediately from theorem 2.12, propositions 2.6 and 2.7, and Chow's theorem 2.1, by induction on the integer $i+j$.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.cor.2.occ3}\textbf{Corollary 2:} \emph{Let $X$ and $Y$ be two nonsingular quasi-projective varieties and $f$ a morphism from $X$ to $Y$. Then the homomorphism $f^!$ from $\underline K(Y)$ to $\underline K(X)$ is compatible with the filtrations, and we have a commutative diagram:}
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large,row sep=large]
G(\underline K(Y)) \arrow[r,"G(f^!)"] & G(\underline K(X))\\
A(Y) \arrow[r,"f^*"] \arrow[u,"\varphi_Y"'] & A(X) \arrow[u,"\varphi_X"'] .
\end{tikzcd}
\]
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 73  |  folio 60  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{60}%
Using the graph of $f$, we decompose $f$ into the product of an \emph{immersion} of $X$ into $X\times Y$ and a \emph{projection} from $X\times Y$ onto $Y$. For the latter the corollary is trivial, and for an immersion we proceed as for corollary 1, using proposition 2.8 in place of proposition 2.7.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.prop.2-13}\textbf{Proposition 2.13 (``cellular decomposition''):} \emph{Let $P$ be an algebraic space (irreducible or not), equipped with an increasing family $P^0\subset P^1\subset\cdots\subset P^n=P$ of closed subsets such that, for every $i$ with $0\leq i\leq n$, the connected components of $P^i-P^{i-1}$ are algebraic spaces $V_{i,\alpha}$ isomorphic to $k^i$ (put $P^{-1}=\varnothing$). Then:}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*)]
\item \emph{For every algebraic space $X$, the natural map from $\underline K(X)\otimes\underline K(P)$ to $\underline K(X\times P)$ deduced from the tensor product of sheaves on $X$ and $P$ is surjective.}
\item \emph{The $\gamma_P(V_{i,\alpha})$ form a system of generators of the group $\underline K(P)$.}
\end{enumerate}
The proof proceeds by induction on $n$, using the corollary of proposition 2.9 and proposition 2.10. (N.B. Unfortunately, I do not know whether the $\gamma_P(V_{i,\alpha})$ form a basis of $\underline K(P)$ and whether the homomorphism considered in a) is bijective. We shall not need to know this in what follows).

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.thm.2-14}\textbf{Theorem 2.14.}
\emph{Let $x_i$ ($1\leq i\leq q$) and $E_j$ ($1\leq j\leq r$) be letters, and $n_j$ ($1\leq j\leq r$) integers $>0$; consider the symbols $\lambda^k(x_i)$ with $k\geq1$ and $\lambda^k(E_j)$ with $1\leq k\leq n_j$ as indeterminates, and let $R$ be a polynomial with integer coefficients in these indeterminates. Assume that, for every graded ring $A$, when in $R$ we substitute for the $x_i$ elements $\xi_i$ and for the $E_j$ elements $\eta_j$ of
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 74  |  folio 61  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{61}%
$\Atil$ subject to the restrictions $\varepsilon(\xi_i)=0$ and $\eta_j=[n_j,1+\eta_j^{(1)}+\cdots+\eta_j^{(n_j)}]$} (thus $\eta_j^{(k)}=0$ for $k>n_j$), \emph{we have}:
\[
 R((\lambda^k(\xi_i)),(\lambda^k(\eta_j)))\in\Atil^p
\]
\emph{Now let $X$ be an irreducible nonsingular quasi-projective variety. For arbitrary elements $x'_i$ of $\underline K(X)^1$ and vector bundles $E'_j$ of dimension $n_j$ on $X$ ($1\leq i\leq q$, $1\leq j\leq r$), we have}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.unnamed}\[
 R((\lambda^k(x'_i)),(\lambda^k(\gamma(E'_j))))\in\underline K(X)^p\qquad\text{\SGAAuthorFootMark{*}}\ .
\]
\SGAAuthorFootText{*}{We readily prove that the hypothesis made implies that, if $R$ is expressed in terms of the $\gamma^k(\xi_i)$, $\gamma^k(E_j-\varepsilon(E_j))$, then all terms of $R$ have weight $\geq p$ (where $\gamma^k$ has weight $k$). Then 2.14 is a particular case of X 1.3.2.}

\emph{Proof}: By \S 2, the $x'_i$ themselves are differences of elements of the form $\gamma(E)$, where $E$ is a vector bundle. On the other hand, assuming $X$ locally closed in a projective space $P$, it follows from \S 2 and proposition 2.5 that, for every $F\in\cF(X)$, the sheaf $F(n)$ is generated by its sections for sufficiently large $n$. If $F$ is defined by a vector bundle $E$, this proves that $E(n)$ is the inverse image of the canonical vector bundle on a Grassmannian variety $G$ under a morphism from $X$ to $G$. Considering the corresponding morphism from $X$ to $P\times G$, we see that $E$ is the inverse image of a vector bundle on $P\times G$. Thus, applying this to all the vector bundles involved, and using corollary 2 of theorem 2.12, we see that \emph{we are reduced to the case where} $X$ \emph{is a product of Grassmannians}. In this case, the conclusion will follow from the following lemma:

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.lem.2-15}\textbf{Lemma 2.15.}
\emph{Assume that $X$ is a product of Grassmannians. Then we can find a $\lambda$-homomorphism from $\underline K(X)$ to a $\lambda$-ring without torsion of the form
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 75  |  folio 62  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{62}%
$\Atil$ (where $A$ is a graded ring), compatible with the filtrations, and such that the corresponding homomorphism $G(\underline K(X))\longrightarrow G(A)$ is injective.}

To prove the lemma, we shall use Chow's theory of Chern classes; a direct proof of the lemma, or of theorem 2.14, would yield a purely ``sheaf-theoretic'' theory of Chern classes (cf.\ \S 5).

We shall consider the homomorphism $\Ctil$ from $\underline K(X)$ to $\Atil(X)$ and put $A=A(X)$; we must prove that the homomorphism $\psi=G(\Ctil)$ from $G(\underline K(X))$ to $G(A(X))\simeq A(X)$ is injective. The fact that $\Ctil$ is compatible with the filtrations holds for every nonsingular quasi-projective variety and follows readily from the corollary to proposition 2.3. On the other hand, as $X$ is a product of Grassmannians $C_j$, we have on $X$ vector bundles $E_j$, inverse images of the canonical bundles on the factors. We shall use the following (known) facts:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*)]
\item On $X$, linear equivalence of cycles is equivalent to numerical equivalence, and $A(X)$ is a group of finite rank;
\item The $C^i(E_j)$ generate the ring $A(X)$.
\end{enumerate}

From a) it follows that $\varphi:A(X)\longrightarrow G(\underline K(X))$ is injective (hence bijective), by the following lemma:

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.lem.2-15.occ2}\textbf{Lemma 2.15.} \emph{Let $X^n$ be a nonsingular projective variety, and $Z^{n-p}$ a cycle on $X$ such that $\varphi(Z)\in G^p(\underline K(X))$ is zero; then $Z^{n-p}$ is numerically equivalent to zero.}

To prove this lemma, it suffices to use the additive function $\chi(F)=\sum_{i\geq0}(-1)^i\dim H^i(X,F)$ and the fact that this function equals
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 76  |  folio 63  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{63}%
$1$
for $F=\underline{O}_{(x)}$ for every $x\in X$.

Thus, in the case considered, $A(X)$ and $G(\underline K(X))$ have the same finite rank in every degree, and $G(\underline K(X))$ has no torsion, since this holds for $A(X)$. From assertion b) above it follows, in a ``formal'' way, that the map $\Ctil$ from $\underline K(X)$ to $\widetilde{A(X)}$ is surjective, modulo finite groups. It readily follows, since $G(\underline K(X))$ and $A(X)$ have the same finite rank in every degree, that the homomorphism $G(\Ctil)$ from $G(\underline K(X))$ to $G(\widetilde{A(X)})$ is bijective modulo finite groups in every degree (use induction on the degree). As $G(\underline K(X))$ has no torsion, this homomorphism $G(\Ctil)$ is therefore injective.
\begin{flushright}q.e.d.\end{flushright}

N.B. The homomorphism $\Ctil$ is not surjective in general, even when $X$ is a projective space of dimension $2$.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.sec.5.occ2}\subsection*{\S 5. Sheaf-theoretic definition of Chern classes. Application to the study of injection morphisms.}

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.thm.2-16}\textbf{Theorem 2.16.} \emph{Let $X$ be a nonsingular quasi-projective variety. Then, for every $x\in\underline K(X)$ and every integer $i\geq0$, the element $\gamma^i(x-\varepsilon(x))$ has filtration $\geq i$. Let $c^i(x)\in G^i(\underline K(X))$ be the class of this element modulo $\underline K(X)^{i+1}$; if we put:}
\[
 \widetilde c(x)=\left[\varepsilon(x),1+\sum_{i\geq1}c^i(x)\right]\in\widetilde{G(\underline K(X))},
\]
\emph{then $\widetilde c$ satisfies the conditions analogous to those of theorem 2.2.}

The first assertion follows from theorem 2.14, as does the fact that $x\longrightarrow\widetilde c(x)$ is a homomorphism of $\lambda$-rings (formula (1.25) implies $\gamma_t(x+y)=\gamma_t(x)\gamma_t(y)$, which already shows that $\widetilde c$ is an additive homomorphism). The ``functoriality'' of $\widetilde c$ is verified immediately, and
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 77  |  folio 64  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{64}%
(2.8) follows from (2.31).
\begin{flushright}q.e.d.\end{flushright}

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.cor.1.occ3}\textbf{Corollary 1:} \emph{We have $c^i(x)=\varphi(C^i(x))$.}

This follows from a uniqueness theorem for Chern classes, proved as in Hirzebruch by passage to flag varieties. We need only show that, if $Y$ is a flag variety associated with a vector bundle on $X$, then $G(\underline K(X))\longrightarrow G(\underline K(Y))$ is injective, which is verified by explicitly determining $\underline K(Y)$ with all its structures from $\underline K(X)$ (I shall not give the details here).

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.cor.2.occ4}\textbf{Corollary 2:} \emph{Assume that, for every element $Z\in A(X)$, there exists a morphism $f$ from $X$ to a product $X'$ of Grassmannians such that $Z$ is in the image of $f^*$. Then the canonical homomorphisms}
\[
 \varphi:A(X)\longrightarrow G(\underline K(X)),\qquad \psi:G(\underline K(X))\longrightarrow A(X),
\]
\emph{satisfy in dimension $i$ the following relations:}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-35}\begin{equation}\tag{2.35}
 \psi\varphi=(-1)^{i-1}(i-1)!\,\underline 1,\qquad
 \varphi\psi=(-1)^{i-1}(i-1)!\,\underline 1.
\end{equation}

As $\varphi$ is surjective, it evidently suffices to prove the first formula, and for this we can assume $X$ a product of Grassmannians. As $\varphi$ is then injective, it suffices to prove the second formula (2.35), which, by corollary 1, is written
\[
 \gamma^i(x-\varepsilon(x))\equiv(-1)^{i-1}(i-1)!\,x\qquad\mathrm{mod}\ \underline K(X)^{i+1}
\]
for every $x\in\underline K(X)^i$. As $\psi$ is \emph{injective}, it suffices to prove the analogous relation for $\Ctil(x)\in\Atil(X)^i$, and this is then a consequence of (1.22 bis).

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.rem.unnamed.occ4}\textbf{Remark:} Corollary 2 makes the validity of (2.35) in all cases plausible; there should be a direct proof of it. In
%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 78  |  folio 65  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{65}%
any case, we readily prove the second formula (2.35) modulo the torsion subgroup of $G(\underline K(X))$, and, in characteristic $0$, this same formula is a consequence of the Riemann--Roch theorem. The interest of (2.35) (when it holds) is that it shows that $\varphi$ is injective modulo torsion groups (perhaps always zero!\SGAAuthorFootnote{*}{No, cf.\ the note on page 30.}), so that we ``do not lose much'' by defining the Chern classes as elements of $G(\underline K(X))$; after taking the tensor product with $\Q$, as we shall do later for the Riemann--Roch theorem, the two definitions of Chern classes would then be identical.

Let $X$ still be nonsingular and quasi-projective; let also $Y$ be a nonsingular subvariety of $X$, and $i$ the injection of $Y$ into $X$. For $i^!$ and $i_!$, we then have the following formulas:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $i^!$ is a homomorphism of $\lambda$-rings
\item $i_!(x.i^!(y))=i_!(x).y$, whence in particular $i_!i^!(y)=\xi y$ with $\xi=i_!i^!(1)=\gamma_X(Y)$,
\item $i^!(i_!(x))=x.\lambda_{-1}(N)$, whence in particular $i^!(\xi)=i^!i_!(1)=\lambda_{-1}(N)$,
\item $i_!(x)i_!(x')=i_!(xx'\lambda_{-1}(N))$,
\end{enumerate}
where, by definition, $N$ is the class in $\underline K(Y)$ of the conormal bundle of $Y$ in $X$. Formulas (i) and (ii) hold for arbitrary morphisms, not necessarily injections, and (iii) and (iv) are verified from definition (2.15) and the analogous formula for inverse images (taking for $x$ and $x'$ classes of vector bundles, and using the fact that
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-36}\begin{equation}\tag{2.36}
 \Tor^{\underline{O}_X}_i(\underline{O}_Y,\underline{O}_Y)=\underline{O}_Y(\wedge^i N).
\end{equation}
)

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 79  |  folio 66  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{66}%
Formula (iv) expresses that $i_!$ is a ring homomorphism when we introduce on $\underline K(Y)$ a new multiplicative structure, by the definition $x_N x'=xx'\lambda_{-1}(N)$ (cf.\ Chapter I, \S 4). It is completed by the formula:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\setcounter{enumi}{4}
\item $i_!(\lambda^p(N,x))=\lambda^p(i_!(x))$ for $p\geq1$,
\end{enumerate}
which means that $i_!$ defines a $\lambda$-homomorphism from $\underline K(Y)_N$ to $\underline K(X)$. This formula (v), certainly true in all cases, has for the moment been proved only in characteristic $0$, since it follows from the conjunction of theorems 1.4 and 2.8.

We have analogous formulas for $i_*$ and $i^*$:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman* bis)]
\item $i^*$ is a homomorphism of graded rings.
\item $i_*(x.i^*(y))=i_*(x).y$, whence in particular $i_*(i^*(y))=\xi' y$, with $\xi'=i_*(1)$,
\item $i^*(i_*(x))=x.(-1)^q N^q$, whence in particular $i^*(\xi')=(-1)^q N^q$,
\item $i_*(x)i_*(x')=i_*(xx'(-1)^q N^q)$,
\item $i_*$ increases degrees by $q$ units.
\end{enumerate}

In these formulas, $q$ is the codimension of $Y$ in $X$, and we put $N^i=C^i(N)$. Only formulas (iii bis) and (iv bis) require a proof; I do not know whether they are well known (or even whether they are true!!!), and merely note that they follow by reduction of formulas (iii) and (iv), \emph{provided that we work in} $G(\underline K(Y))$ \emph{and} $G(\underline K(X))$ \emph{and not in} $A(Y)$ \emph{and} $A(X)$. We then use the fact that $i_*$ and $i^*$ arise by passage to the quotient from $i_!$ and $i^!$, noting that $i_!$ shifts the filtration by $q$ units.

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 80  |  folio 67  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{67}%
N.B. All the ``universal'' formulas relating $i_!$ and $i^!$ (resp.\ $i_*$ and $i^*$) are formal consequences of the preceding ones, as one can convince oneself without much difficulty.

From formula (v) follows the formula
\[
 i_!(\gamma^p(N,x))=\gamma^p(i_!(x)).
\]
The conjunction of theorem 2.14 and proposition 1.5 shows that $\gamma^p(N,x)$ has, for $p=q+j$, filtration $\geq j$; denoting by $G_p(N,x)$ its class in $G^j(\underline K(Y))$, theorem 2.16 shows that we have
\[
 i_*(G_p(N,x))=c^p(i_!(x)).
\]
Finally, proposition 1.5 together with theorem 2.14 allows us to express $G_p(N,x)$ as a polynomial with integer coefficients in the $c^i(N)$, the $c^i(x)$ and $\varepsilon(x)$; we deduce the theorem:

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.thm.2-17}\textbf{Theorem 2.17.} \emph{Assume that the field $k$ has characteristic $0$ and let $i:Y\longrightarrow X$ be an injection morphism of nonsingular quasi-projective varieties. We then have formulas (i) to (v), and, moreover, for every integer $j\geq0$, the formula:}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-37}\begin{equation}\tag{2.37}
 c^{q+j}(i_!(x))=i_*\left(\frac{\widetilde c(x)*\lambda_{-1}(\widetilde c(N))}{(-1)^qN^q}\right)^{(j)}.
\end{equation}

The right-hand side of formula (2.37) is an abbreviated way of denoting the polynomial in the components of $\widetilde c(x)$ and $\widetilde c(N)$ described in proposition 1.5.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.cor.unnamed.occ5}\textbf{Corollary:} \emph{Under the preceding conditions, we have:}
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-38}\begin{equation}\tag{2.38}
 \ch(\widetilde c(i_!(x)))=i_*\left(\ch(\widetilde c(x))\,\mathcal T(\check N)^{-1}\right)
\end{equation}
\emph{where $\ch$ is the Chern homomorphism defined by formula} (1.26).

%%%%%%%%%%  scan idx 81  |  folio 68  %%%%%%%%%%
\SGAPrintPage{68}%
It suffices to use (2.37) written in the form:
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-37bis}\begin{equation}\tag{2.37 bis}
 \widetilde c(i_!(x))=1+i_*\left(\frac{\widetilde c(x)*\lambda_{-1}(\widetilde c(N))-1}{(-1)^qN^q}\right),
\end{equation}
the explicit formula (1.29), formula (iv bis), and finally (1.30).

We can write (2.38) in the form
\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.eq.2-38bis}\begin{equation}\tag{2.38 bis}
 \ch_X(F)=i_*\left(\ch_Y(F)\,\mathcal T(T_{X/Y})^{-1}\right),
\end{equation}
where $F$ is a coherent algebraic sheaf on $Y$ (considered on the left-hand side as a sheaf on $X$ zero outside $Y$), $\ch_X$ and $\ch_Y$ are the Chern homomorphisms taken respectively on $X$ and $Y$, and $T_{X/Y}$ is the normal bundle of $Y$ in $X$.

\sgaStableTarget{sga6.e00.rem.unnamed.occ5}\textbf{Remarks:} Unlike (2.38), formula (2.37) is a ``formula without denominators''. It has been proved (even in characteristic $0$) only by working in $G(\underline K(X))$ and not in $A(X)$, which may not be the same thing. It is certainly true in every characteristic and in $A(X)$. Let us note that we immediately verify that the images of the two sides under $i^*$ are the same, whence it follows, on applying $i_*$, that the product of their difference by $\xi'$ is zero. This suggests a completely different method of proof, by trying to obtain $Y$ as the transverse inverse image of a subvariety $Y'$ in a variety $X'$ such that, in dimension $\leq n=\dim X$, the class of $Y'$ in $A(X')$ is not a divisor of $0$\dots. Unfortunately, we cannot hope that $X'$ is a Grassmannian or something close to one.

We shall note that, if we disregard torsion elements, formula (2.38) is equivalent to (2.37).




